I"�<p>E-learning statistics are useful when they answer a specific institutional question. Participation, learning outcomes, market forecasts and business returns measure different things; one should not be used as proof of another.</p>

<p>This guide uses US government distance-education data and an identified online-learning research review. The sources were checked on 4 October 2026. Their observation periods remain explicit, rather than presenting older projections as measured current results.</p>

<h2 id="distance-education-participation-a-defined-population">Distance-education participation: a defined population</h2>

<p>The National Center for Education Statistics’ <strong>Digest Table 311.15</strong> reports fall 2024 enrollment at US degree-granting postsecondary institutions. “Distance education” is the category used by that dataset; it is not a count of every learner using a digital tool.</p>

