<p>The “some” and “exclusively” counts sum to the “any distance education” count. Do not add all three as if they describe separate groups. Rounded percentages can differ slightly from calculations using the published counts.</p>

<p>These figures establish participation within a defined US higher-education population. They do not establish course quality, student satisfaction, global adoption or an institution’s financial return.</p>

<h2 id="what-research-says-about-learning-outcomes">What research says about learning outcomes</h2>

<p>The US Department of Education’s <strong>2009 Evaluation of Evidence-Based Practices in Online Learning</strong> synthesised <strong>51 study effects</strong>, mostly involving older learners. The report found better average outcomes in the included online-learning conditions, with a stronger advantage in blended conditions.</p>

<p>The report also warns against attributing the difference to the delivery medium alone. Conditions could differ in learning time, materials and instructional approaches. It is historical evidence about the included studies—not a promise that moving any course online improves results.</p>

<p>For a new programme, assess the learning design, learner support and assessment evidence. Do not substitute a general research result for evaluation of your own course.</p>

<h2 id="trends-worth-evaluating-without-invented-growth-claims">Trends worth evaluating without invented growth claims</h2>

<h3 id="flexible-delivery">Flexible delivery</h3>

<p>Distance participation makes flexible access an important design question. Decide which activities can be asynchronous and which require supervised practice, collaboration or assessment. Flexibility should preserve the programme’s learning and accessibility requirements.</p>

<h3 id="blended-learning">Blended learning</h3>

<p>Define the role of each delivery mode. Keep the intended outcomes, assessment criteria and additional learning time visible when comparing a blended programme with another format.</p>

<h3 id="learner-support-and-accessibility">Learner support and accessibility</h3>

<p>Test device access, connectivity, accessible content and support arrangements with the intended learners. An enrollment figure does not tell you whether those conditions are adequate for your population.</p>

<h3 id="evidence-after-course-completion">Evidence after course completion</h3>

<p>Completion records, assessed competence and learner-facing credentials serve different purposes. Decide what the institution is authorised to award and which evidence belongs with it.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-measure-programme-value">How to measure programme value</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Decision question</th>
      <th>Measure to define</th>
      <th>What not to infer</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Are learners participating?</td>
      <td>Enrollment, active participation and a defined completion measure</td>
      <td>Participation alone does not establish competence.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Did learners meet the outcomes?</td>
      <td>Assessment method, criteria and demonstrated achievement</td>
      <td>A delivery platform is not proof of learning quality.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Can the programme operate sustainably?</td>
      <td>Implementation, content, support and ongoing costs</td>
      <td>A quoted market-growth forecast is not programme ROI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Is the award useful after completion?</td>
      <td>Issuer authority, evidence, skills relationships and record use</td>
      <td>Sharing activity is not an employment guarantee.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>A reported proportion of organisations experiencing a revenue increase is not the size of that increase. Without the original cohort, period and method, neither should become a blanket ROI claim in a procurement case.</p>

